      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Tarrowby Logistics, a fictional company. You're covering the recruitment inbox this morning, and six messages are waiting. Some are real applications and some were built to trick you. Sort them, and report anything that doesn't sit right. Reporting a real message never costs you anything.",
        "flipHeading": "Now it lands on you",
        "flipIntro": "This is the application you just sent, as Tarrowby's recruitment team would see it. The parts picked out below are what make it believable.",
        "flipDefenderLine": "Jun in HR opened it between two interviews, with forty applications still to read. It looked like one more CV for the warehouse job, so he opened the attachment and did what it asked. People who fall for messages like this are not stupid: this one was built to look right.",
        "flipNoSend": "Your application didn't go out this time, so there's nothing of yours to show. Next, the same kind of message arrives in a recruitment inbox, and you're the one opening it.",
        "noBlameLine": "Nobody is marked down for a miss here. Every one of these messages was built to look right, and opening attachments from strangers is part of a recruiter's job. The people who get caught out are busy, not careless.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: read CVs in the viewer, and if a document asks you to enable editing or content, don't. If a CV is 'too big to attach' and sits behind a link that wants you to sign in, ask the candidate to send it as an attachment instead. Then report anything odd the way your organisation asks you to.",
        "defendOnly": "You went straight to the inbox this time. These messages were written by people whose job is to make them look right, and people who fall for messages like these are not stupid. Each card shows what gave the fake ones away, and why the real ones were fine.",
        "conceptPanel": {
          "heading": "Why 'enable content' matters",
          "text": "Office documents can carry macros, small programs that run inside the file. Many organisations now block macros in files that arrive from the internet, so this trick works less often than it used to. The lure still turns up, though, because it only has to work once. Reading a CV in the viewer is always enough; a real CV never needs you to switch anything on."
        }
      },
      "debrief": {
        "mondayCard": "slot:customer_report_route",
        "noBlameLine": true
      }
    }
